   "subject": "Factor Score Estimated with EBM and ML methods: sd differences",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Dear all,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">I have a problem that has been puzzling me for the past week.</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">I am simulating 50 runs of a model and using the output of the lavaan function to estimate factor scores which I was expecting to be something close to N(0,1). Since I am using binary indicators, I use the methods &quot;EBM&quot; and &quot;ML&quot; and I ran a check to see if their estimates were close. To my surprise, the standard deviation from the ML method was something around 2x, 3x times the one observed with &quot;EBM&quot;. </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Below is the code just before calling lavInspect(). fit measures are good enough,i.e., TLI and CLI close to 1 and rmsea close to 0. </div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan/attach/c24ac85927fa/image.png?part=0.0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"520\" height=\"250\"><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">The boxplot for the 50 runs for the 2 methods is as below (also attached). Is there any theoretical explanation for this disparity in sd? Note the difference on the scale of the y axis!</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan/attach/c24ac85927fa/image.png?part=0.0.2&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"564\" height=\"420\" style=\"margin-right:25px\"><br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan/attach/c24ac85927fa/image.png?part=0.0.3&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"537\" height=\"402\" style=\"margin-right:25px\"><br></div></div><div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Looking forward to hearing from you,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Hugo</div><br></div>-- <br><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><div><br><table width=\"100%\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:&quot;Times New Roman&quot;;font-size:medium\"><tbody><tr><td align=\"left\" valign=\"middle\" width=\"10\"><p style=\"margin-right:10px;font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:12px;line-height:14px\"><a href=\"http://www.adaptativa.com.br/\" style=\"text-decoration:none\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.adaptativa.com.br/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792421689785000&amp;usg=AOvVaw12KXJK3r8uoC11-yDz0sPi\"><img src=\"https://ci4.googleusercontent.com/proxy/FE2I4wjFnN2cAFKRmI3TKMszOlSp21wnNu4SqBeCJZBTRvT_FvrlgmwCaxvFTesKdXjysxxb7nvH4if6T_hi884vh3N59BHtgm3-wqx9bgkcnTo-Jw1Bg01c1Tk3qGCKYDUC3lEyIIXfgYCyCshNyEk3Ee8Wg4ibOcS9FfxhtyTI=s0-d-e1-ft#https://htmlsigs.s3.amazonaws.com/logos/files/000/177/391/landscape/adaptativa_256px80pxL_72dpi_SEM_SLOGAN.jpg\" alt=\"Adaptativa\" border=\"0\" height=\"62\" width=\"200\"></a></p></td><td align=\"left\"><p style=\"font-family:Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;line-height:14px;color:rgb(33,33,33)\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold;display:inline\">Hugo Harada</span><span style=\"display:in